<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Psamtik I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 664–610 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Psammetichus I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Psamtik I was the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Twenty-sixth, or Saite, Dynasty, reigning from 664 to 610 BCE. Beginning as an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, he reunited Egypt, threw off Assyrian control and relied on Greek and Carian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, opening Egypt to Greek traders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says his army besieged Ashdod for 29 years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/psamtik-i.jpg" alt="Psamtik I" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_from_Statue_of_a_King_MET_EGX.358.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In 671 BCE the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> against the Kushite rulers of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty and set up twelve Delta kinglets, a Dodecarchy, allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-i">Necho I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>, the most powerful of them. In 665 BCE the Kushite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> again. Necho I died in battle and his son Psamtik fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. In 664 BCE Ashurbanipal, Esarhaddon's son, retook Memphis, sacked Thebes and expelled Tantamani, and Psamtik returned with the Assyrian force and was installed as ruler of Sais and Memphis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Psamtik I was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Twenty-sixth Dynasty, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> between 664 and 610 BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the other kinglets chased him from Memphis into the Delta swamps after he fulfilled an oracle by pouring a libation from a bronze vessel. From 662 BCE he made contact with Gyges, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, who sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> and Carian mercenaries, with whom he reconquered Memphis and defeated the other kinglets. He placed mercenaries in garrisons at Daphnae and Elephantine, and by 657 BCE he held the Delta.</p><p>In 656 BCE he sent an expedition to Thebes that made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun">God's Wife of Amun</a>, Shepenupet II, adopt his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitocris-i">Nitocris I</a> as heiress. In 655 and 654 BCE he fought Libyan tribes and put a garrison at Marea. Herodotus describes a twenty-nine year siege of Ashdod, though its dating is uncertain. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> died in 627 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> unravelled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> reached as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, and Psamtik persuaded them with gifts to turn back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Psamtik died in 610 BCE and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a>. Herodotus reports that Psamtik tried to find the origin of language by giving two newborn children to a shepherd, who was told to let no one speak to them. When one child cried bekós, Psamtik concluded that the word was Phrygian, the Phrygian word for bread, and that Phrygian was the original language. No other source confirms the story.</p><p>In March 2017 Egyptian and German archaeologists found a colossal quartzite statue about 7.9 metres tall at Heliopolis, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. It was first thought to depict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, but an inscription with one of Psamtik's names identified it as his.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Psamtik I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herodotus tells that Protothyes’ son Madyes defeated the Medes, that the Scythians ruled Asia for twenty-eight years, raiding as far as Palestine, where the pharaoh <b>Psamtik I</b> bought them off, and that the Median king Cyaxares at last killed their leaders at a feast.</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Ashdod remained an Assyrian vassal, survived a long Egyptian siege under <b>Psamtik I</b>, and lost its kings to Nebuchadnezzar II of the Chaldean Empire, who deported them to Babylon c. 604 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashdod">Ashdod</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Psamtik I</b>, son of the Saite ruler Necho I, began in 664 BCE as an Assyrian vassal after Assyria drove out the Kushite Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
